<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for Bosun (required by notarization).

  One capability entitlement, no hardened-runtime exceptions. Why nothing else is needed:
    - libghostty is *statically* linked into the Bosun Mach-O, not a separate
      dylib, so there is nothing to validate against — `disable-library-validation`
      is unnecessary.
    - The terminal exec's a login shell via posix_spawn, which hardened runtime
      allows without any entitlement.
    - Metal shaders are precompiled at build time (the Metal Toolchain step), so
      no runtime JIT / writable-executable memory is required.

  iCloud key-value sync (#83): connections + folders sync via NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore,
  which requires the ubiquity-kvstore entitlement below. Carries no secrets: the synced
  payload is connection metadata only (SSH auth stays in ssh-agent/keys).

  The Team ID is written literally rather than as `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)`. That
  variable is substituted by *Xcode* before it invokes codesign, and this is a SwiftPM
  build with no Xcode in the loop. Verified: signing with the real Developer ID identity
  embeds the literal string `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)...` into the signature, with no
  warning, even though TeamIdentifier is recorded correctly. Keep in sync with the team
  of whatever SIGN_IDENTITY package-app.sh is given.

  NOT YET FUNCTIONAL: ubiquity-kvstore is a *restricted* entitlement, so macOS honours it
  only when an embedded.provisionprofile grants it. That requires a registered App ID with
  the iCloud capability, a Developer ID provisioning profile, and a step in package-app.sh
  copying it to Contents/embedded.provisionprofile before the codesign call. None of that
  exists yet. The failure is silent: codesign, notarization, and Gatekeeper all pass and
  KVS simply never syncs.

  If a hardened-runtime launch ever faults with a code-signing error (e.g. an
  EXC_BAD_ACCESS in a JIT/W^X path), add the *minimal* exception here and re-test:
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key><true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key><true/>
  Each added key widens the attack surface, so add only what an actual fault proves
  is needed — verified by launching the hardened-runtime-signed app, not by guessing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
	<string>FGVMQ5BBGM.dev.anvas.bosun</string>
</dict>
</plist>
